{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"BATCHING EVIDENCE — measured against the landed renderer (merged `cf5155e528`) over the committed 178-body corpus. This bears on this story's central mechanism, so recording it before any further plan iteration.\n\n## Run-length distribution (what batching could ever save)\n233 contiguous SAFE runs across 884 `_RENDER` units. **106 of 233 runs (46%) are a single unit.**\n```\nlen  1: 106 runs      len  6:  11      len 11:   4\nlen  2:  15           len  7:  14      len 12:   4\nlen  3:  22           len  8:  10      len 13:   6\nlen  4:  13           len  9:   6      len 14:   3\nlen  5:  15           len 10:   3      len 15:   1\n```\nTheoretical best case: 233 spawns instead of 884 = **74% fewer**. That is the ceiling, only reachable if every run batches successfully.\n\n## Two candidate designs, both prototyped and measured\n**(A) Run-level fallback (what the plan currently specifies — lift the preservation check to the run).** A single bad fragment poisons the whole run, degrading every unit in it to raw Markdown: **63 of 178 bodies (35%) lose richness** versus the landed per-unit renderer. Unacceptable.\n\n**(B) Verify-then-accept, per-unit fallback.** Batch the run; accept the batched wiki only if the preservation check passes; otherwise re-render that run through the LANDED per-unit path. Measured: **157/178 identical** to per-unit, but only **775 vs 884 spawns = 12% fewer** — far below the 74% ceiling, because a failed batch pays BOTH the batch call and the per-unit calls.\n\n## The reviewer's semantic concern is CONFIRMED\nEven under (B), **21 of 178 bodies still render differently** from the per-unit path when the batch verifies successfully. pandoc parses block boundaries relative to surrounding content, so concatenating units into one commonmark document is genuinely not equivalent to rendering them separately (setext-style headings, lazy continuation, and reference-style links are the usual culprits). Batching is therefore not a transparent optimization — it changes output.\n\n## The question this raises\nAs specified, safe-run batching buys ~12% fewer spawns while changing output on ~12% of bodies. The hazard it was introduced to fix is the ~0.3s/field spawn tax (~20 min of wall time on a large serial reconcile). Options:\n1. **Descope batching** from this story, keeping the timeout + process-group reaping + fallback + compliance work (which are independently valuable and uncontested), and let the spawn tax be addressed by the cutover story's change-gated `rich_sha` render gate — which already avoids rendering unchanged bodies entirely, and is a much larger win than 12%.\n2. **Keep batching under design (B)** and accept that ~12% of bodies render differently from today's landed output, with an AC pinning that difference as intended.\n3. **Investigate the 21 divergent bodies** to find a document-assembly rule (e.g. forcing a hard block separator) that restores equivalence, then re-measure.\n\nThis exceeds what should be decided unilaterally — the story's stated mechanism does not deliver its stated benefit — so it is going back to the operator/driver rather than being silently redesigned. Work on `disparaged-parodistic-shrew` continues meanwhile; it is unblocked and independent of this decision."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786596554936172001,"uuid":"5cae87d7-dcb6-49f2-a4a0-63a905b062c1"}